#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#: README:2
msgid "INTRODUCTION::"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:9
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      The scripts in this directory implement an automation of the \n"
"   building of a GNU/Linux system, as described in the Linux From \n"
"   Scratch book series. The name of the project is jhalfs: in that name, \n"
"   \"alfs\" stands for \"automated linux from scratch\", and the initials \n"
"   \"jh\" have been kept since the original \"jhalfs-0.2\" code developed by \n"
"   Jeremy Huntwork.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:14
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      The list of supported books can be found at \n"
"   http://wiki.linuxfromscratch.org/alfs/wiki/SupportedBooks (maybe \n"
"   outdated, current develoment books and latest version are always \n"
"   supported).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:17
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      The documentation is split among various README.* files. Here is a \n"
"   list of what is in which:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '      - '
#: README:22
msgid ""
"README (this file): instructions to use the LFS book. This should be enough "
"if you just want to build a base system as per the LFS book. It is also a "
"required reading for all the other projects."
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '      - '
#: README:28
msgid ""
"README.BLFS: instructions to install an automated build infrastructure for "
"the BLFS book. There are two ways to do so: (i) install the tools at the end "
"of an LFS build, or (ii)  install the tools on an already running system. "
"Both methods are described in that file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '      - '
#: README:33
msgid ""
"README.CUSTOM: instructions to run custom commands either during the LFS "
"build, or at the end of a LFS build. Note that you will not find "
"instructions on how to write those commands, but some examples are available."
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '      - '
#: README:37
msgid ""
"README.PACKAGE_MANAGEMENT: instructions to use package management during the "
"build (Note: the only package manager that is regularly tested is porg)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:41
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Other sources of information are the context help in the menu \n"
"   interface, and the LFS books themselves (both required readings of \n"
"   course!).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#: README:43
msgid "PREREQUISITES::"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:46
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      It is strongly advised that you first build manually a complete \n"
"   system before attempting to automate the build.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:50
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      In addition to a good knowledge of UNIX (linux), the ability to \n"
"   read, understand and modify shell scripts may be required at some \n"
"   point.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:53
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Since the XML book sources are used by this tool, and those are \n"
"   kept in a git repository, a basic knowledge of git may help.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:60
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Of course the \"Host System Requirements\" should be fulfilled. Some \n"
"   supplementary packages are needed for using jhalfs. They are detailed \n"
"   at the bottom of the page: \n"
"   https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/alfs/download.html. In short, you \n"
"   need wget, sudo, libxml2, libxslt, docbook-4.5-xml, and \n"
"   docbook-xsl-nons.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '3. '
#: README:62
msgid "INSTALLATION::"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:66
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      No installation is required. You may want to move the files in \n"
"   this directory to a convenient location, and then follow the \n"
"   instructions below.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '4. '
#: README:68
msgid "CONFIGURATION::"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:70
#, no-wrap
msgid "   4.1. CONFIGURATION OF THE TOOLS:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:74
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"         There is no configuration of the tools themselves. The various \n"
"      parameters for the build are set through a menu driven interface. \n"
"      See the section RUNNING below for details.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:76
#, no-wrap
msgid "   4.2. PRELIMINARY TASKS:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:84
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"         This tool has no support at all for creating a partition and a \n"
"      mount point for the built system. You should follow the book up to \n"
"      the section \"Mounting the new partition\". Note that the default \n"
"      name for the partition mount point is \"/mnt/build_dir\", instead of \n"
"      /mnt/lfs. You can change that default to anything you'd like in \n"
"      the menu, so you may name it /mnt/lfs if you prefer . We'll use \n"
"      the name /mnt/build_dir in the sequel.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:93
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"         For downloading packages, you can use the tool or download them \n"
"      yourself. Even if using the tool, it is recommended to set up a \n"
"      source repository where you store already downloaded packages. The \n"
"      tool will automatically search a package in this repository before \n"
"      downloading it if it is not found there. This repository cannot be \n"
"      the same as /mnt/build_dir/sources. As an example, we'll use \n"
"      /usr/src. You should arrange for the user running the tool to have \n"
"      write access to this directory.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:102
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"         If you want to build the kernel as part of the automated build, \n"
"      a configuration file must be provided. In order to do so, it is \n"
"      recommended to download the kernel tarball, unpack it, run <make \n"
"      menuconfig> (or any other *config), configure the kernel as per \n"
"      the book, and save the resulting .config file to a location where \n"
"      it can be retrieved later on. It is suggested to put it into the \n"
"      source repository, with a versioned name, e.g. \n"
"      /usr/src/config-<arch>-<kernel version>-<config details>.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:109
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"         Another file you may provide is the fstab file. As for the \n"
"      kernel configuration, this file has to be prepared before running \n"
"      the menu. You can copy-paste the file from the \"Creating the \n"
"      /etc/fstab File\" page, then edit to suit the future lfs system \n"
"      layout, then save the file. A convenient location and name is \n"
"      /usr/src/fstablfs.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:115
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"         At a more advanced level, you may want to supply custom \n"
"      commands to be run at the end of LFS build. Scripts containing \n"
"      those commands are located in the ./custom/config directory. \n"
"      Examples are given in ./custom/examples. A template is provided as \n"
"      ./custom/template. See README.CUSTOM for more details.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '5. '
#: README:117
msgid "RUNNING::"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:119
#, no-wrap
msgid "   IMPORTANT::\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:125
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"         You must be logged as a normal user with sudo privileges to run \n"
"      the Makefile. Furthermore, you are supposed to have enough \n"
"      privilege to become any user. If you are not bothered about \n"
"      security issues, the entry for the user running the tool in \n"
"      /etc/sudoers could be <user> ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD:ALL\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:128
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      The command <make> will launch a menu based configuration program, \n"
"   similar to the kernel \"menuconfig\" configuration tool.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:133
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Help on parameter function is available from the on-line help \n"
"   (type the character `?' after highlighting the parameter). Please do \n"
"   use the help: it may contain additional information not duplicated in \n"
"   this file.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:135
#, no-wrap
msgid "   MENU \"BOOK Settings\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:138
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Choose BOOK: You have two choices: \"Linux from scratch\" or \"Beyond \n"
"      Kinux From Scratch (see help)\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:140
#, no-wrap
msgid "         The BLFS part is described in README.BLFS\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:143
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Init system: You have two choices: \"LFS SysV\" or \"LFS systemd\" (or \n"
"      same with BLFS if you have chosen it above)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:145
#, no-wrap
msgid "         Allows to choose between the two flavours of the books.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:148
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      XML source of the Book: Two choices: \"retrieved from git \n"
"      repository\" or \"Local Copy\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:152
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"         The first choice is more flexible, since it will allow to \n"
"      choose any point in git history. But the second one allows to make \n"
"      local changes to the source.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:155
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Which point in git history? (if you have chosen to retrieve the \n"
"      book sources from the git repository)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:160
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"         You can use any way of denote a commit known to the \"git\" \n"
"      program. Most popular ones are \"trunk\" (for the development \n"
"      version) or the version string of a stable version (such as \n"
"      \"12.1\").\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:163
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Location of local copy (mandatory) (if you have chosen the local \n"
"      copy)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:166
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"         This is the absolute path to the local copy of the book \n"
"         sources.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:169
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Multilib: Four choices: Normal LFS, Multilib with i686 libraries, \n"
"      multilib with x32 libraries, multilib with all libraries.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:172
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"         It is recommended to use \"Normal LFS\" unless you know what you \n"
"      are doing\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:174
#, no-wrap
msgid "      Build method: two choices: chroot (as in book), boot\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:177
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"         Presently, the \"boot\" method is not implemented, so keep the \n"
"         default.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:179
#, no-wrap
msgid "      Add blfs-tools support (see README.BLFS)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:183
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"         This will install the blfs tools onto the newly built system. \n"
"      It is not the same thing as choosing the BLFS book in the menu, \n"
"      which will install the blfs tools on the currently running system.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:185
#, no-wrap
msgid "      Add custom tools support (see README.CUSTOM)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:187
#, no-wrap
msgid "   MENU \"General Settings\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:189
#, no-wrap
msgid "      Temporary user for chapters 4, 5, and 6 (should not exist):\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:193
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"         the name of the user running the commands in those chapters. In \n"
"      the book, it is named lfs, and this is the default. You can change \n"
"      it to any non existing user.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:195
#, no-wrap
msgid "      Group of user for chapters 4, 5, and 6 (should not exist):\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:198
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"         this is lfs in the book, but you can rename it to any non \n"
"      existing group name.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:200
#, no-wrap
msgid "      Build Directory: the name of the root of the LFS system\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:204
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"         This is the equivalent of the LFS variable in the book. Set it \n"
"      to \"/mnt/lfs\" if you have followed the book for creating the LFS \n"
"      partition and mount point.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:209
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Retrieve source files: Say y to have jhalfs download the packages \n"
"         If you say no, you must download the packages yourself and put\n"
"      them into the /mnt/build_dir/sources directory. Follow book's\n"
"      chapter 3 instructions.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:211
#, no-wrap
msgid "         If you say yes, you'll be asked several other questions:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '         - '
#: README:213
msgid "Package Archive Directory: Repository of downloaded packages"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:217
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            This directory, which is on the host and should be writable \n"
"         by the user running the tool, is for storing downloaded \n"
"         packages.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:222
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            If you keep the default \"$SRC_ARCHIVE\", you can set this \n"
"         variable to the absolute path of the repository and export it. \n"
"         Or if the variable is not set, jhalfs downloads the sources \n"
"         directly to /mnt/build_dir/sources.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:225
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Instead of using the SRC_ARCHIVE envar, you can also enter \n"
"         the path of the repository directory into this field.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '         - '
#: README:227
msgid "Retry on 'connection refused' failure: self explanatory"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:230
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"         - Number of retry attempts on download failures: self \n"
"         explanatory\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '         - '
#: README:232
msgid "Download timeout (in seconds): self explanatory"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:235
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Run the makefile: start the build immediately after running the \n"
"      tool\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:241
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"         This is not the preferred method: it is recommended to rather \n"
"      run \"make -C /mnt/build_dir/jhalfs\" after the tool has finished \n"
"      setting up the build. But this may be handy if you are sure \n"
"      everything is well, and want to leave the tool and the build run \n"
"      without supervision.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:243
#, no-wrap
msgid "      Rebuild files: clean up the /mnt/build_dir directory\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:249
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"         Say n if you want to rerun the tool (to update generated \n"
"      scripts for example) without removing what has already been done. \n"
"      Otherwise, say y. Note that there are some guards against removing \n"
"      a directory containing useful things, but double check that the \n"
"      /mnt/build_dir directory is really what you want to erase.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:251
#, no-wrap
msgid "   MENU \"Build Settings\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:253
#, no-wrap
msgid "      MENU Parallelism settings\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '       - '
#: README:255
msgid "Use all cores:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:263
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            If you say y, MAKEFLAGS will be set to \"-j$(nproc)\" at the \n"
"         beginning of each script. Other envars are supposed to be \n"
"         passed from the environment, as done in new books. Note that \n"
"         for old books, this means the scripts using make or ninja will \n"
"         be run with all cores, but not when this needs to set special \n"
"         envars like TESTSUITEFLAGS. You can still define the number of \n"
"         cores used in next field.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:266
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            If you say n, you'll be asked for a static number of threads \n"
"         to use.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:269
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"       - set of cpus to use, or 'all' for all cpus (only if using all \n"
"       cores):\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:273
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            You can define here the cores you want to use. See help for \n"
"         details. This is the preferred way of reducing the number of \n"
"         cores rather than using a static thread number.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '       - '
#: README:275
msgid "Number of parallel `make' jobs (only if not using all cores):"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:282
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            Every occurrence of $(nproc) in new books will be replaced \n"
"         with the number entered here. Also MAKEFLAGS will be set to \n"
"         \"-jN\" (where N is the number entered) at the beginning of each \n"
"         scripts. Furthermore NINJAJOBS will be set to N in the \n"
"         environment. This allows to run all books with N threads, \n"
"         except for paarts that need other envars to be set\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '       - '
#: README:284
msgid "Build Binutils pass1 without parallelism (Real SBU)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:289
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"            The standard SBU is defined as the time to run the \n"
"         binutils-pass1 build with only one thread. Saying y here allows \n"
"         to get a value for it. If you say n, the value is not \n"
"         meaningful for SBU measurements.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:291
#, no-wrap
msgid "      Run testsuites: say y to run the test suites\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:294
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"         You'll have the choice between running all the test suites, or \n"
"      only those deemed critical (binutils, gmp, mpfr, mpc, and gcc).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:296
#, no-wrap
msgid "      Package management: see README.PACKAGE_MANAGEMENT\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:298
#, no-wrap
msgid "      Create a log of installed files for each package: self explanatory\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:301
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      Strip Installed Binaries/Libraries: use the book instructions for \n"
"      stripping\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: README:303
#, no-wrap
msgid "      DO NOT use/display progress_bar (self explanatory)\n"
msgstr ""

